{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-2-158j-spring-2003", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["2.158J"], "cross_listed_codes": ["1.128J", "16.940J"], "course_title": "Computational Geometry", "course_slug": "2-158j-computational-geometry-spring-2003", "term": "Spring", "year": 2003, "as_taught_in": "Spring 2003", "language": "en", "departments": ["Mechanical Engineering", "Civil and Environmental Engineering", "Aeronautics and Astronautics"], "academic_levels": ["Graduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Nicholas Patrikalakis", "Prof. Takashi Maekawa"], "subject_primary": "Computer Science", "subject_secondary": ["Algorithms and Data Structures", "Graphics and Visualization", "Mathematics", "Computation", "Topology and Geometry"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Engineering", "subtopics": [{"name": "Computer Science", "specialties": ["Algorithms and Data Structures", "Graphics and Visualization"]}]}, {"name": "Mathematics", "subtopics": [{"name": "Computation", "specialties": []}, {"name": "Topology and Geometry", "specialties": []}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Lecture Notes"], "description": "Topics in surface modeling: b-splines, non-uniform rational b-splines, physically based deformable surfaces, sweeps and generalized cylinders, offsets, blending and filleting surfaces. Non-linear solvers and intersection problems. Solid modeling: constructive solid geometry, boundary representation, non-manifold and mixed-dimension boundary representation models, octrees. Robustness of geometric computations. Interval methods. Finite and boundary element discretization methods for continuum mechanics problems. Scientific visualization. Variational geometry. Tolerances. Inspection methods. Feature representation and recognition. Shape interrogation for design, analysis, and manufacturing. Involves analytical and programming assignments